<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Final exam problems on vectors
-->
<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-exercises-vectors" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Exercises for <xref ref="chapter-vectors" text="title"/></title>

<introduction>
<p>
These problems add and subtract vectors given by their endpoints. If you would like to review this topic before
attempting them, see <xref ref="chapter-vectors" text="title"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise xml:id="rs19-2">
<statement>
<p>
Write each combination of vectors as a single vector. See
<xref ref="fig-rs19-quad"/>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>\vec{PQ} + \vec{QR}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\vec{QS} - \vec{PS}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\vec{RS} + \vec{SP} + \vec{PQ}</m>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rs19-quad">
<caption>
The quadrilateral with vertices <m>P</m>, <m>Q</m>, <m>R</m>, and
<m>S</m>, with its diagonals.
</caption>
<image width="52%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-rs19-quad">
<diagram dimensions="(300,240)" margins="12">
<coordinates bbox="(-0.6,-0.55,3.9,3.5)">
<line at="qd-QP" endpoints="((0.8,2.9),(2.9,2.2))"
      stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2"/>
<line at="qd-PS" endpoints="((2.9,2.2),(3.3,0.3))"
      stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2"/>
<line at="qd-RS" endpoints="((0,0),(3.3,0.3))"
      stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2"/>
<line at="qd-RQ" endpoints="((0,0),(0.8,2.9))"
      stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2"/>
<line at="qd-QS" endpoints="((0.8,2.9),(3.3,0.3))"
      stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="qd-RP" endpoints="((0,0),(2.9,2.2))"
      stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="1.5"/>
<point at="qd-P" p="(2.9,2.2)" fill="black"/>
<point at="qd-Q" p="(0.8,2.9)" fill="black"/>
<point at="qd-R" p="(0,0)" fill="black"/>
<point at="qd-S" p="(3.3,0.3)" fill="black"/>
<label at="qd-P-lab" anchor="(3.0,2.32)" alignment="ne">
<m>P</m>
</label>
<label at="qd-Q-lab" anchor="(0.72,3.02)" alignment="nw">
<m>Q</m>
</label>
<label at="qd-R-lab" anchor="(-0.12,-0.12)" alignment="sw">
<m>R</m>
</label>
<label at="qd-S-lab" anchor="(3.42,0.22)" alignment="e">
<m>S</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A quadrilateral with vertices labelled Q at the top left, P at the top right, R at the bottom left, and S at the bottom right, drawn in light blue together with its two diagonals Q S and R P.">
  <annotation ref="qd-P" text="The vertex P at the top right."/>
  <annotation ref="qd-Q" text="The vertex Q at the top left."/>
  <annotation ref="qd-R" text="The vertex R at the bottom left."/>
  <annotation ref="qd-S" text="The vertex S at the bottom right."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
By the triangle law, placing <m>\vec{QR}</m> at the tip of
<m>\vec{PQ}</m>,
<md>
\vec{PQ} + \vec{QR} = \vec{PR}.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Since <m>-\vec{PS} = \vec{SP}</m>,
<md>
\vec{QS} - \vec{PS} = \vec{QS} + \vec{SP} = \vec{QP}.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Chaining the three vectors tip to tail,
<md>
\vec{RS} + \vec{SP} + \vec{PQ} = \vec{RQ}.
</md>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-9">
<statement>
<p>
Find a vector of length 3 pointing in the opposite direction from the
vector <m>2\,\mathbf i - 4\,\mathbf j + 4\,\mathbf k</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The given vector has length
<m>\sqrt{4 + 16 + 16} = \sqrt{36} = 6</m>, so the unit vector in its
direction is <m>\frac16\langle 2, -4, 4\rangle
= \left\langle \frac13, -\frac23, \frac23\right\rangle</m>.
Multiplying by <m>-3</m> reverses the direction and gives length
<m>3</m>:
<md>
-3\left\langle \frac13, -\frac23, \frac23\right\rangle
= \langle -1, 2, -2\rangle
= -\mathbf i + 2\,\mathbf j - 2\,\mathbf k.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-10">
<statement>
<p>
The vectors <m>2\,\mathbf i + 3\,\mathbf j</m>,
<m>4\,\mathbf i + \mathbf j</m> and <m>5\,\mathbf i + y\,\mathbf j</m>
have their tails at the origin. Find the value of <m>y</m> that will
make their heads collinear.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The heads are the points <m>(2,3)</m>, <m>(4,1)</m>, and
<m>(5,y)</m>. The line through the first two has slope
<md>
m = \frac{1-3}{4-2} = -1.
</md>
For <m>(5,y)</m> to lie on that same line, the slope from
<m>(4,1)</m> to <m>(5,y)</m> must also be <m>-1</m>:
<md>
\frac{y-1}{5-4} = -1 \;\Rightarrow\; y - 1 = -1
\;\Rightarrow\; y = 0.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

</worksheet>
